=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1, dswa2
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** nftables FORWARD chain on dswa1 and dswa2 (the Office A distribution switch VRRP pair), specifically a two-rule sequence injected onto their `br0.10` VLAN-10 bridge interface.

**Mechanism:** Both dswa1 and dswa2 carry an identical policy in their active `type filter hook forward` chain:
1. `iifname "br0.10" meta l4proto icmp ip saddr 10.1.0.0/24 ip daddr 10.3.0.0/24 → ACCEPT`
2. `iifname "br0.10" ip saddr 10.1.0.0/24 ip daddr 10.3.0.0/24 → DROP`

ICMP packets from Office A (10.1.0.0/24) destined for Office B (10.3.0.0/24) match rule 1 and are accepted. All other protocols (TCP, UDP) fall through to rule 2 and are silently dropped. This is why the Phase 1 ICMP-based reachability probe showed 0% loss — pings succeed, masking the real fault. The ICMP ACCEPT counter on dswa1 recorded exactly 6 packets (from our diagnostic tests), while the DROP counter remains 0 because only ICMP was tested.

**Localization:** dswa1 (`br0.10`, 10.1.0.2/24) and dswa2 (`br0.10`, 10.1.0.3/24) — both Office A distribution switches. Traffic from any Office A host returning to Office B traverses one of these two switches on `br0.10` and is dropped if it is not ICMP.

**Root cause:** Maintenance personnel injected an ACL on dswa1 and dswa2 that permits only ICMP while dropping all other traffic flowing from Office A (10.1.0.0/24) back to Office B (10.3.0.0/24). This breaks TCP handshakes (SYN-ACK is dropped), UDP flows including DNS responses, and every application-level service — explaining the user's experience of being cut off from everything despite ICMP pings succeeding.

**Fix:** Remove both rules from the FORWARD chain on dswa1 and dswa2: `nft delete rule ip filter FORWARD` for both the ICMP ACCEPT and the blanket DROP targeting `ip saddr 10.1.0.0/24 ip daddr 10.3.0.0/24`.
=== END ===